  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Local Humanitarian Leadership in Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project &quot;Humanitarian System Transformation through Local Humanitarian Leadership&quot; (HST LHL) funded by Dutch Ministry of Foreign Affairs aims to deliver the following outcomes: Outcome 1: More Equitable and Locally Led Humanitarian System: focuses on empowering local humanitarian actors by strengthening their capacity, diversifying funding, and creating opportunities for involvement in leadership platforms. It aims to establish a joint agenda for advocacy, increasing visibility for local leadership, and fostering mutual accountability. Outcome 2: More Risk -Informed and Safe Humanitarian System: seeks to develop evidence -based, risk -informed approaches and scalable initiatives to reduce humanitarian risks. It addresses risks faced by humanitarian actors and communities, emphasizing the need for shared responsibility in risk management. The focus is on challenging power imbalances and ensuring equal partnership in the humanitarian delivery chain. This outcome advocates for long -term investment in managing risks rather than responding to crises. Outcome 3: Increased Quality and Quantity of Humanitarian Funding Managed by LNHAs: explores ways to increase the quality and quantity of funding for local and national humanitarian actors (LNHAs). It focuses on securing investments from governments, climate funding, and innovative financial mechanisms to improve the effectiveness and inclusivity of humanitarian response. It aims to ensure timely and accountable funding for preparedness, resilience, and response, connecting local communities to global advocacy campaigns to secure financial and political support.</narrative>
  </description>
